.TH OROC-CONFIG 1 "" "Oro Runtime 0.1.2" "Oro Runtime Manual"
.SH NAME
oroc\-config \- Inspect configuration values.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
oroc config [options] [<key\-or\-path>]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Inspect configuration values.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-config=<path>\fR
use an explicit oro.toml/oro.ini file
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
list known configuration keys with current and default values
.TP
\fB\-\-key=<name>\fR
print the current value for a specific key
.TP
\fB\-\-describe=<name>\fR
print help and metadata for a specific key
.TP
\fB\-f, \-\-format=<format>\fR
print the full configuration as 'toml', 'ini', or 'json'
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
shorthand for '\-\-format=json'
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
treat unknown or unset keys as errors (non\-zero exit)
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-file=<path>\fR
mirror logs to a JSON file
.SH NOTES
.PP
Keys may be provided in flattened form (e.g., "filesystem_sandbox_enabled") or as TOML\-style paths (e.g., "filesystem.sandbox_enabled"). A bare argument after 'config' is treated as a key query (for example, 'oroc config filesystem.sandbox_enabled'). Unknown keys are printed when present in the active configuration but are marked as undocumented. `\-\-format` prints the merged effective configuration after applying oro.toml, local .ororc overrides, and CLI\-selected config sources. Use '\-\-log\-file=<path>' instead of the global 'oroc \-\-json ...' flag when you need structured config output on stdout and logs at the same time.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBoroc config \-\-list\fR
list all known configuration keys with effective values
.TP
\fBoroc config meta.version\fR
print the effective value of meta.version
.TP
\fBoroc config \-\-describe webview.root\fR
print the description, default, and source for webview.root
.TP
\fBoroc config "ios.*"\fR
glob\-match a group of related keys
.TP
\fBoroc config \-\-format json\fR
print the merged effective configuration as JSON
.SH SEE ALSO
oroc(1)
